  {
   "id": "32DVWc_dAAAJ",
   "from": "Daniel Sommerhoff",
   "author": "0cc91f41491ff09e",
   "date": "2018-08-21T11:45:03Z",
   "subject": "items of type \"none\"",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Phil,<div><br></div><div>I&#39;m trying to create a multi-dim CAT and most things work like a charm, but I have a question regarding the inclusion of type &quot;none&quot; questions.</div><div>Following Scenario:</div><div>I want to create a CAT with two blocks with 50 questions each and an &quot;instructions&quot;-Page between block1 and block2. To include the instructions page, I created a type &quot;none&quot; question with an item stem that consists of the instructions for block2. Thus, I created an according data.frame for the questions with 101 rows, rows 1:50 belonging to the questions of block 1, row 51 being the type none instruction page, and rows 52:101 belonging to the questions of block 2. This works like a charm for a non-adaptive,sequential test.</div><div>However, if I want to create an adaptive test with an underlying model (based on prior responses to the 100 items), I have to define an underlying model that should look mostly like this:</div><div><br></div><div>&quot;F1 = 1-50</div><div> F2 = 52-101&quot;<br></div><div><br></div><div>However, if I use this model, I have to provide a dataset with answers for 101 questions and using an &quot;NA&quot;-Column for the type none question 51 does not work, giving me an error message (see example code). Still, leaving the type none item out while creating the model also does not make sense.</div><div><br></div><div>So how to proceed with type none-questions when creating a mirt-model?</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks in advance,</div><div><br></div><div>Daniel </div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>Example Code (generating random responses for questions 1:50 and 52:101) and trying to create the mirt-model afterwards</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><font color=\"#660066\"><div>x&lt;-c(rep(1,100),rep(0,100))</div><div>V1&lt;-sample(x,100)</div><div>for (i in 1:100){</div><div>  V1&lt;-cbind(V1,sample(x,100))</div><div>}</div><div>data&lt;-V1</div><div>data&lt;-cbind(data[,1:50],NA,data[,51:100])</div><div>colnames(data)&lt;-1:101</div><div><br></div><div>model &lt;- mirt.model(&quot;F1 = 1-50</div><div>                     F2 = 52-101&quot;)</div><div>mirt(data,model,guess=0.25, method=&quot;EM&quot;,technical=list(NCYCLES=15))-&gt;mirt_model</div></font></div></code></div><br><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "KNr4gBUvAQAJ",
   "from": "Phil Chalmers",
   "author": "b330c2028fed415b",
   "date": "2018-08-21T20:11:27Z",
   "subject": "Re: items of type \"none\"",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Daniel,<div><br></div><div>I understand what you are asking, and can certainly offer some advice here, but want to make sure I get the intention right first. To start, do you have real data that corresponds the CAT items you are trying to make? In other words, do you already have access to a dataset that has 100 items (whereas your CAT requires 101 item structures, as you are aware of)?</div><div><br></div><div>If so, then fit the data normally using the mirt() function, and translate these parameters into the structure you need via the generate.mirt_object() function. That way, you know that these &quot;ghosted&quot; items are not included in the fitted model, and so that you have complete control over where these &#39;none&#39; types go during the new model translation definition. Admittedly, this is a bit awkward, and I&#39;m open to suggestions on how to improve this format. HTH.</div><div><br clear=\"all\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_signature\" data-smartmail=\"gmail_signature\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Phil</div></div></div></div><br></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\">On Tue, Aug 21, 2018 at 7:45 AM Daniel Sommerhoff &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">daniel.s...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Phil,<div><br></div><div>I&#39;m trying to create a multi-dim CAT and most things work like a charm, but I have a question regarding the inclusion of type &quot;none&quot; questions.</div><div>Following Scenario:</div><div>I want to create a CAT with two blocks with 50 questions each and an &quot;instructions&quot;-Page between block1 and block2. To include the instructions page, I created a type &quot;none&quot; question with an item stem that consists of the instructions for block2. Thus, I created an according data.frame for the questions with 101 rows, rows 1:50 belonging to the questions of block 1, row 51 being the type none instruction page, and rows 52:101 belonging to the questions of block 2. This works like a charm for a non-adaptive,sequential test.</div><div>However, if I want to create an adaptive test with an underlying model (based on prior responses to the 100 items), I have to define an underlying model that should look mostly like this:</div><div><br></div><div>&quot;F1 = 1-50</div><div> F2 = 52-101&quot;<br></div><div><br></div><div>However, if I use this model, I have to provide a dataset with answers for 101 questions and using an &quot;NA&quot;-Column for the type none question 51 does not work, giving me an error message (see example code). Still, leaving the type none item out while creating the model also does not make sense.</div><div><br></div><div>So how to proceed with type none-questions when creating a mirt-model?</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks in advance,</div><div><br></div><div>Daniel </div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>Example Code (generating random responses for questions 1:50 and 52:101) and trying to create the mirt-model afterwards</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><font color=\"#660066\"><div>x&lt;-c(rep(1,100),rep(0,100))</div><div>V1&lt;-sample(x,100)</div><div>for (i in 1:100){</div><div>  V1&lt;-cbind(V1,sample(x,100))</div><div>}</div><div>data&lt;-V1</div><div>data&lt;-cbind(data[,1:50],NA,data[,51:100])</div><div>colnames(data)&lt;-1:101</div><div><br></div><div>model &lt;- mirt.model(&quot;F1 = 1-50</div><div>                     F2 = 52-101&quot;)</div><div>mirt(data,model,guess=0.25, method=&quot;EM&quot;,technical=list(NCYCLES=15))-&gt;mirt_model</div></font></div></code></div><br><br></div></div>\r\n\r\n<p></p></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">\r\n\r\n-- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;mirt-package&quot; group.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">mirt-package...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nFor more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/optout\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/optout&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792473368760000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1UML8JsPHW8ywD41xDpKWO\">https://groups.google.com/d/optout</a>.<br>\r\n</blockquote></div>\r\n"
    ]
   ]
